Single-database configuration for Flask.

## 2026-09-18 — migration history reset

The previous migration chain was broken: `b1d2c3e4f5a6_add_video_podcasts.py` and
`f3a9c1d7e2b8_drop_user_phone_address.py` each pointed at a `down_revision` that no
longer existed in this directory (`797c143dcaa8`, `49e0870a5bd6`), so `flask db upgrade`
could not provision a fresh database. Those files (plus their two dependents,
`c9b3f2a1e7d4_add_wordpress_blog_cache_fields.py` and
`e4d7a9c2b601_add_wordpress_video_cache_fields.py`) have been replaced with a single
autogenerated baseline, `d39f20095b92_baseline_consolidated_schema_from_.py`
(`down_revision = None`), generated from the current SQLAlchemy models in
`app/models.py`. It was verified against an empty SQLite database: `flask db upgrade`
creates all 21 tables, and a follow-up `flask db migrate` reports no schema drift.

**Any database that already has this schema (staging, production) must be stamped,
not upgraded**, so Alembic records it as already being at the new baseline without
re-running the `CREATE TABLE` statements:

```
flask db stamp d39f20095b92
```

Only a genuinely empty database (a new environment, CI, local dev) should run
`flask db upgrade` against this baseline directly.
